<section id="upgrade">
<h2>Upgrade the daemon pair and console together</h2>
<p>Keep the previous binaries and configuration available for rollback. Recent releases changed conversation journals and memory settings; older journals reset rather than migrate automatically. <a href="/whats-new/#migration">Review the migration checklist</a> before restarting an existing installation.</p>
</section>
<section id="configure">
<h2>The broker needs a provider and an explicit grant</h2>
<p>The gateway needs a chat transport, model endpoint and agent catalog. The broker needs explicitly installed providers plus identity, policy and execution constraints. The container includes only the in-tree <code>cli-probe</code> and <code>http-probe</code> components; install reviewed out-of-tree providers yourself. Supply credentials only to their owning process and configure telemetry collection before giving the agent useful access.</p>
<p>The <a href="{{ site.repository }}/blob/main/examples/slack-github/README.md">Slack GitHub reviewer recipe ↗</a> provides a pinned provider and complete read-only configuration for two people in one channel. Start with the Agent manifest; classic is an optional workspace fallback. The <a href="/deploy/mac/#pat">Mac PAT instructions</a> explain the broker's private credential file: launchd does not load <code>.env</code> automatically.</p>
<p>Validate authored files with <code>dekopon-gatewayd check gatewayd.yaml --catalog agents.d</code> and <code>dekopon-brokerd check broker.yaml</code> before deployment. A managed broker check also needs <code>--provider-set</code> and a private <code>--store</code>; it may fetch its pinned components. Ordinary checks don't prove that the socket, credentials or external services work. The gateway's explicit <code>check --probe</code> can make a paid OpenRouter canary request. Managed deployment sets also need <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#providers">sync and precompile</a>.</p>
<p><a class="button button-primary" href="/deploy/mac/">Run on a Mac →</a> <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/">Kubernetes installation</a>
</p>
<p>Application <a href="{{ release.url }}">{{ release.tag }}</a> and the chart are independently versioned. Both Linux arm64 and amd64 images are published; source availability is not evidence of a healthy deployment.</p>
<p>The release archives and Linux image also include <a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-console/README.md"><code>dekopon-console</code> ↗</a>, an optional operator client. Homebrew installs it separately with <code>brew install dekopon-agents/tap/dekopon-console</code>. The console does not support OpenRouter models and is not needed for the Mac Slack path. The chart can run it idle for <a href="/deploy/kubernetes/#pod"><code>kubectl exec</code></a>. To write a provider, use the <a href="/guides/provider-sdk/">SDK guide</a>.</p>
</section>
